# mmBERT Tibetan Text Boundary Detector

Fine-tunes [jhu-clsp/mmBERT-base](https://huggingface.co/jhu-clsp/mmBERT-base) (ModernBERT architecture, 307 M params, 8192-token context) for token classification to detect text boundaries in Tibetan Buddhist texts.

## Data format

Each file in `data/Annotated_jsons/` is a JSON with three keys:

| Key | Type | Description |
|-----|------|-------------|
| `filename` | string | Volume identifier (e.g. `W123_I456_001`) |
| `content` | string | Full UTF-8 text of the volume |
| `segments` | list | Objects with `span_start`, `span_end`, and `label` |

Boundary positions are derived as the `span_start` of every segment after the first (each segment transition is a boundary).

## Setup

```bash
python -m venv .env
source .env/bin/activate
pip install -e .
```

This installs the `mmbert-boundary` command and all dependencies.

## Workflow

### Step 1 — Prepare data

> Full guide: [docs/01-prepare-training-data.md](docs/01-prepare-training-data.md)

Groups documents by BDRC work ID, deduplicates, and splits at the work level so no volumes of the same collected work leak across train/val/test. Tokenizes each document into sliding windows (default 8192 tokens, 256-token stride) and assigns B/O labels based on boundary character offsets.

```bash
mmbert-boundary prepare-data
```

Quick test with fewer works and smaller windows:

```bash
mmbert-boundary prepare-data --max-works 20 --max-length 512 --stride 128
```

Point at a custom data directory:

```bash
mmbert-boundary prepare-data --data-dir /path/to/Annotated_jsons
```

**Outputs** (in `data/processed/`):

| File | Contents |
|------|----------|
| `dataset/` | HuggingFace DatasetDict with train/validation/test splits |
| `split_info.json` | Work IDs and doc IDs per split |
| `data_config.json` | Tokenization parameters, label counts |

### Step 2 — Sample benchmark (optional)

> Full guide: [docs/02-sample-benchmark.md](docs/02-sample-benchmark.md)

Creates a fixed benchmark subset from the test split, stratified by boundary density, for quick and consistent comparisons across experiments. This is not a second train/val/test split — it only subsets docs already held out as test.

```bash
mmbert-boundary sample-benchmark
mmbert-boundary sample-benchmark --num-docs 30 --max-windows 500
```

**Outputs** (in `data/benchmark/`):

| File | Contents |
|------|----------|
| `dataset/` | HuggingFace Dataset of sampled test windows |
| `benchmark_meta.json` | Doc IDs and density stats used by `evaluate --on-benchmark` |

### Step 3 — Train

> Full guide: [docs/03-train.md](docs/03-train.md)

Fine-tunes mmBERT with focal loss to handle the extreme class imbalance (~1:700 B:O ratio). Supports MPS (Apple Silicon), CUDA, and CPU.

```bash
mmbert-boundary train
```

Key flags:

```bash
mmbert-boundary train \
  --epochs 5 \
  --batch-size 2 \
  --grad-accumulation 8 \
  --lr 2e-5 \
  --eval-steps 500 \
  --save-steps 500
```

Resume from a checkpoint:

```bash
mmbert-boundary train --resume output/checkpoints/best
```

Recommended H100 config:

```bash
mmbert-boundary train \
  --batch-size 8 --grad-accumulation 2 --epochs 5 \
  --eval-steps 200 --save-steps 600 --focal-gamma 3.0 \
  --neg-sample-ratio 0.03 --patience 15
```

Enable [Weights & Biases](https://wandb.ai) experiment tracking:

```bash
mmbert-boundary train --wandb
mmbert-boundary train --wandb --wandb-project my-project --wandb-run-name exp-focal-g3
```

**Outputs** (in `output/`):

| Path | Contents |
|------|----------|
| `checkpoints/best/` | Best model by validation F2 |
| `checkpoints/final/` | Model after last epoch |
| `checkpoints/checkpoint-{step}/` | Periodic saves |
| `training_log.json` | Per-step metrics |
| `training.log` | Full stdout/stderr log |

**Tuning tips:**

- Low recall (missing boundaries): increase `--pos-weight` to 15–20 (CE path only — use `--no-focal-loss`)
- Low precision (too many false positives): decrease `--pos-weight` to 5 (CE path only)
- Out of memory: reduce `--batch-size` to 1, increase `--grad-accumulation` proportionally

### Step 4 — Evaluate

Converts token-level predictions back to character positions, then matches against ground truth with configurable character tolerance.

```bash
mmbert-boundary evaluate --model output/checkpoints/best
```

Use the benchmark subset:

```bash
mmbert-boundary evaluate --model output/checkpoints/best --on-benchmark
```

Adjust tolerance:

```bash
mmbert-boundary evaluate --model output/checkpoints/best --tolerance 20
```

Reports micro/macro precision, recall, F1 per document and in aggregate. Full report saved to `output/eval_report.json`.

### Step 5 — Predict on new text

Run inference on raw Tibetan text files:

```bash
# Single file
mmbert-boundary predict input.txt --model output/checkpoints/best

# Save results as JSON
mmbert-boundary predict input.txt --model output/checkpoints/best --output boundaries.json

# Save annotated text with <b> markers
mmbert-boundary predict input.txt --model output/checkpoints/best --annotated output_annotated.txt

# Batch process a directory
mmbert-boundary predict docs_folder/ --model output/checkpoints/best --output results/
```

Adjust confidence threshold (default 0.70):

```bash
mmbert-boundary predict input.txt --model output/checkpoints/best --threshold 0.3
```

## Project structure

```
pyproject.toml
README.md
requirements.txt
src/mmbert_boundary/
  __init__.py            # public API and version
  config.py              # shared defaults (all overridable via CLI flags)
  cli.py                 # unified mmbert-boundary entry point
  core/
    prepare.py           # Step 1: data prep with work-level dedup
    benchmark.py         # Step 2: benchmark sampling
    train.py             # Step 3: fine-tuning
    evaluate.py          # Step 4: evaluation
    predict.py           # Step 5: inference
  utils/
    device.py            # get_device()
    seed.py              # set_seed()
    logging.py           # tee stdout/stderr for training
    collate.py           # shared DataLoader collate functions
data/
  Annotated_jsons/       # source per-volume JSONs
  processed/             # tokenized datasets (generated)
  benchmark/             # benchmark subset (generated)
output/
  checkpoints/           # saved models (generated)
  training_log.json      # training metrics (generated)
```

## Configuration

All defaults live in `src/mmbert_boundary/config.py`. Every value can be overridden via the corresponding CLI flag.

| Parameter | Default | Description |
|-----------|---------|-------------|
| `MODEL_NAME` | `jhu-clsp/mmBERT-base` | Base model |
| `MAX_SEQ_LENGTH` | `8192` | Sliding window size in tokens |
| `STRIDE` | `256` | Window overlap |
| `LEARNING_RATE` | `2e-5` | Peak learning rate |
| `NUM_EPOCHS` | `5` | Training epochs |
| `FOCAL_GAMMA` | `1.5` | Focal loss gamma |
| `FOCAL_ALPHA` | `0.90` | Focal loss alpha for the B class |
| `NEG_SAMPLE_RATIO` | `0.20` | Fraction of O-only windows retained |
| `TOLERANCE_CHARS` | `25` | Char tolerance for eval matching |
| `SEED` | `42` | Random seed |

## Python API

```python
from mmbert_boundary import predict_boundaries, annotate_text
from transformers import AutoModelForTokenClassification, AutoTokenizer
import torch

model = AutoModelForTokenClassification.from_pretrained("output/checkpoints/best")
tokenizer = AutoTokenizer.from_pretrained("output/checkpoints/best")
device = torch.device("cpu")

text = open("input.txt").read()
boundaries = predict_boundaries(text, model, tokenizer, device)
annotated = annotate_text(text, boundaries)
```
